{"skill":{"slug":"turix-cua","displayName":"TuriX Computer Use"},"version":{"version":"1.0.8","createdAt":1770776648817,"changelog":"Change the display name","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"README.md","size":2462,"sha256":"080c86a5d0db12de436ddfa00f13f74fd72870d1a8fe504a5d5d04a0a8a4bce9","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"scripts/run_turix.sh","size":5427,"sha256":"17ea35987ddceacc92708b8ae5f0f27b3e0fab17128a2fc9f93b103ae99e5863","contentType":"application/x-sh"},{"path":"SKILL.md","size":12922,"sha256":"71548a07888289915858b78af2d7098331951b4edcf149806ec768439e1f4a4b","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2151,"sha256":"421b0726ba1a6a60153c1e1c64ad6ae3e28357d50329782667bbc45afbf69dd2","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789024184310,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"a5ce137bd1218f6527ec2ca88a4b9481be56d398a6ca3a2341c51d86be575759","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/a5ce137bd1218f6527ec2ca88a4b9481be56d398a6ca3a2341c51d86be575759","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: turix-cua\nVersion: 1.0.8\n\nThe skill bundle is designed for macOS GUI automation, a powerful but legitimate function. It transparently requests high-level permissions (Screen Recording, Accessibility) necessary for its operation. Crucially, the `run_turix.sh` script employs a Python helper to safely update the `config.json` with task arguments, effectively mitigating potential shell injection vulnerabilities. While the script broadly modifies the PATH, this appears to be for functional purposes (e.g., locating `screencapture`) rather than malicious intent. There is no evidence of prompt injection against the agent or code designed for data exfiltration, persistence, or other malicious activities.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779160369799},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":60,"severity":"HIGH","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":7,"checkedAt":1789024184310},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"The skill is a coherent macOS desktop automation helper, but it asks for broad screen-control permissions and relies on unpinned external code that defines the real behavior.","dimensions":[{"detail":"The README tells users to grant Screen Recording and Accessibility to broad general-purpose apps and runtimes such as Terminal, VS Code, Node, and python3, which is broader than a narrowly scoped automation component and expands the impact of unrelated code run through those tools.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"Setup delegates the core implementation to the external TurixAI/TuriX-CUA repository and pip requirements without a pinned commit, checksum, lockfile, or version validation, so the reviewed package does not fully determine what code users will run.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill encourages autonomous desktop workflows, including email/upload examples and a README example involving Google account sign-up, without clear boundaries for authentication, personal accounts, payments, messaging, admin prompts, or other sensitive UI actions.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"There is no evidence of a hidden service or backdoor, but macOS privacy permissions persist until revoked, the launcher rewrites the selected config file in place, and TuriX stores task logs/records under .turix_tmp.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The artifacts consistently describe TuriX-based visual control of macOS apps, clicking, browsing, sending files, planning, resume, logs, and config updates; those capabilities fit the stated desktop automation purpose but are inherently high impact.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"note"}],"guidance":"Review this skill carefully before installing. Use it only in a dedicated, low-sensitivity macOS session, avoid account sign-ups, OAuth, financial, messaging, admin, or confidential-data workflows unless you are supervising each step, pin and review the exact TuriX-CUA commit and dependency set, grant the narrowest possible Screen Recording and Accessibility permissions, and revoke those permissions after use.","findings":"[T05] unexpected: Desktop automation needs screen observation and UI control, but the artifact-backed guidance to authorize multiple general-purpose runtimes is overbroad and under-scoped.\n[T08] unexpected: Using TuriX-CUA is central to the skill, but leaving the upstream repository and Python dependencies unpinned means the effective executable behavior can change after review.\n[SQP-2] unexpected: The Google account sign-up example is supported by the README and is a poor fit for an unsafeguarded desktop automation demo because it involves identity-linked account actions.\n[SQP-2] unexpected: The broad desktop-control capability is disclosed, but the artifacts do not prominently warn users that visible sensitive data and authenticated sessions may be observed or acted on.\n[SQP-2] expected: The config rewrite finding is partly mitigated because SKILL.md explicitly says run_turix.sh updates examples/config.json; however, the script also supports --config and rewrites that file without a separate confirmation.\n[LP3] unexpected: The package has no clear permission boundary or tool scope for a high-impact desktop-control skill, even though some operational safeguards such as a force-stop hotkey are documented.\n[AR2] expected: This scanner concern is not supported as anti-refusal behavior; the quoted phrase appears in guidance to avoid tasks requiring system-level permissions without warning.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789024184310}}}}}